This directory vendors the NeoRL SalesPromotion user model and its transition logic.

Source: https://github.com/polixir/NeoRL, branch `benchmark` (commit 1c2853d,
2024-11-21), directory `neorl/neorl_envs/SalesPromotion/sales_promo/`.
Paper: Qin et al., "NeoRL: A Near Real-World Benchmark for Offline
Reinforcement Learning", NeurIPS 2022 Datasets and Benchmarks.
Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0; see LICENSE (identical to
upstream).

Vendored files:
- `env/marketing.py` -> `sales_promotion/marketing.py`
- `models/env_model.py`, `models/__init__.py`, `models/torch/*.py` ->
  `sales_promotion/models/` (an empty `models/torch/__init__.py` was added so
  the directory is a regular package)
- `env/user_model.pt`, `env/test_initial_states_10000_people.npy`,
  `env/evaluation_init_obs.npy` -> `sales_promotion/assets/` (unchanged)
Not vendored: `env/sp_env.py` (the `gym` wrapper; the projeto-rl adapter
`projeto_rl/envs/sales_promotion.py` reproduces its observable-state update and
reward), `env/env_instance.py`, and the rest of NeoRL.

Local modifications:
- `marketing.py`: no `gym`, `neorl.core`, or `tqdm` imports; `MarketingEnv`
  is a plain class. The constructor takes a `torch.Generator` and a
  `numpy.random.RandomState` instead of an optional seed that set the global
  torch and numpy states; every `torch.bernoulli` and `torch.randn` of the
  user rules uses that generator. `step` also returns the per-user cost and
  GMV arrays (the original returned only the totals). The debug `print`, the
  deprecated `_forloop_step`, the gym space setters, `seed`, and
  `validation_length` were removed.
- `models/torch/policy_cont.py`, `models/torch/policy_disc.py`,
  `models/env_model.py`: `select_action` takes the generator (keyword
  `generator`) and samples with `torch.normal` / `torch.multinomial` on it,
  which draw exactly as the original `Normal.sample()`,
  `Categorical.sample()`, and `Tensor.multinomial(1)`;
  `EnsembleVenvModel.select_action` also takes `np_rng` for the ensemble
  member choice (the original used `np.random.randint`).
With the same seeds, the port reproduces the original `MarketingEnv.step`
exactly (checked on 200 users over 5 days: maximum absolute difference 0.0 in
costs, GMV, user actions, and states).
